<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lucretia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lucretia was, according to Roman tradition, a noblewoman whose rape by Sextus Tarquinius, son of the king, and her subsequent suicide provoked the overthrow of the Roman monarchy in 509 BCE. Her story, told by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, became a celebrated subject of later literature and art, though historians regard it as legend.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lucretia.jpg" alt="Lucretia" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Artemisia Gentileschi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artemisia_Gentileschi_-_Lucretia_-_J._Paul_Getty_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
